<%# Follow / block control for `user` (the profile owner being viewed). Self-wraps
    in dom_id(user, :follow_button) so the follow/block Turbo Streams replace it in
    place. Only shown to a signed-in non-owner. button_to lives on the main routes
    (CSRF on) so the generated form carries the authenticity token.
    States (Slice 7): blocked-by-me → Unblock (never Follow); blocked-by-them →
    Block only (never Follow for a hidden pair); otherwise Follow/Following + Block.

    DESIGN SYSTEM (Slice 9, Task 4.4; restyled Hoojah 2026 Phase 2.4) —
    docs/design-system/components/social/FollowButton.prompt.md: "the follow/block pair
    on a profile header." Phase 2.4 needed this same control on a card-background search
    result row, so the single hard-coded look below split into a `surface:` local with
    two treatments:

      `surface: :gradient` — the saturated blue/gradient profile hero. Reuses
      `ds_button_classes(variant: :on_primary / :on_primary_outline)` UNCHANGED: those
      two variants are locked to white-on-primary and the small size precisely because
      there is exactly one surface they may appear on (see design_system_helper.rb),
      and this is still it. They take no `tone:` — asking for one is a category error.

      `surface: :card` (the DEFAULT) — a card/white background. `ds_button_classes`'s
      own outline variant is a heavier weight (2px border, shadow) built for a
      page-level CTA, not a compact result row, so `:card` is NOT drawn from
      `ds_button_classes` at all — `filled_classes`/`outline_classes` below spell it
      directly: a solid primary pill for "the relationship exists", a thin
      primary-outline pill on `bg-card` for "act".

    Every EXISTING caller (`_profile_header`, `_gated_header`, the four follow/block
    turbo_stream views) passes no `surface:`, so they now render `:card` by default —
    intended by this restyle, not a regression to patch. Both header partials are
    still `bg-primary` (the gradient hero itself is a separate, not-yet-landed piece of
    Hoojah 2026 — see HANDOVER's deferred backlog "Follow button on hero"), so those two
    callers will look visibly plain until that hero lands and switches them to
    `surface: :gradient`.

    Filled means "the relationship exists": Following, Requested, Unblock. Outlined
    means "act": Follow, Block. That split is unchanged by `surface:` — only the two
    concrete class strings it maps to move.

    "Requested" is FULLY opaque, where on `:gradient` it used to be a translucent
    white. That is a design decision, not something the variant forced: `on_primary`
    plus an opacity utility in `class:` was available and is exactly what the Block
    chip does three branches down. The reason is that FollowButton.prompt.md names the
    clock glyph and the word "Requested" as the signal for a pending request — a state
    the viewer must be able to read and cancel, not a faded version of a button.
    Translucency was carrying meaning the icon and label already carry, at the cost of
    legibility. `filled_classes` on `:card` carries no opacity either, for the same
    reason.

    The secondary Block that rides alongside Follow/Following carries `opacity-80` to
    keep it quieter than the control it sits next to, on either surface. Opacity is the
    one utility that can express that without fighting `outline_classes`'s own border:
    a border-opacity or padding override would be the SAME Tailwind family as the
    border/padding already in `outline_classes`, and same-family utilities resolve by
    bundle order rather than call order (see `ui/_card`'s `padded:` header for the full
    trap). Neither `outline_classes` branch sets a base opacity, so `opacity-80` has
    nothing to collide with.

    FROZEN: every `button_to` path and HTTP verb, on BOTH surfaces. `unfollow_user_path`
    with DELETE is reused by BOTH "Following" (drop an accepted follow) and "Requested"
    (cancel a pending one) — that is deliberate, not a copy-paste. `dom_id(user,
    :follow_button)` is the replace target for all four of follows/blocks create+destroy
    turbo streams. %>
<% surface = local_assigns.fetch(:surface, :card) %>
<% filled_classes, outline_classes = case surface
   when :gradient
     [ds_button_classes(variant: :on_primary), ds_button_classes(variant: :on_primary_outline)]
   when :card
     ["px-4 py-1 text-sm rounded-full bg-primary text-white",
       "px-4 py-1 text-sm rounded-full border border-primary text-primary bg-card"]
   else
     raise ArgumentError, "unknown surface #{surface.inspect}; expected :card or :gradient" unless Rails.env.production?
     [ds_button_classes(variant: :on_primary), ds_button_classes(variant: :on_primary_outline)]
   end %>
<div id="<%= dom_id(user, :follow_button) %>">
  <% if user_signed_in? && current_user.id != user.id %>
    <% if current_user.blocks_made.exists?(blocked_id: user.id) %>
      <%= button_to unblock_user_path(user.username), method: :delete,
            class: filled_classes do %>
        <%= lucide_icon("user-x", class: "w-4 h-4") %>
        <span>Unblock</span>
      <% end %>
    <% elsif current_user.hidden_user_ids.include?(user.id) %>
      <%# They blocked me — never offer Follow. Block-back stays available. %>
      <%= button_to block_user_path(user.username),
            class: outline_classes do %>
        <%= lucide_icon("ban", class: "w-4 h-4") %>
        <span>Block</span>
      <% end %>
    <% else %>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
        <% if current_user.following.include?(user) %>
          <%= button_to unfollow_user_path(user.username), method: :delete,
                class: filled_classes do %>
            <%= lucide_icon("user-check", class: "w-4 h-4") %>
            <span>Following</span>
          <% end %>
        <% elsif current_user.active_follows.pending.exists?(followed_id: user.id) %>
          <%# Private target with a pending request (Slice 7b). The button cancels the
              request — reuses the unfollow route (DELETE removes the pending follow). %>
          <%= button_to unfollow_user_path(user.username), method: :delete,
                class: filled_classes do %>
            <%= lucide_icon("clock", class: "w-4 h-4") %>
            <span>Requested</span>
          <% end %>
        <% else %>
          <%= button_to follow_user_path(user.username),
                class: outline_classes do %>
            <%= lucide_icon("user-plus", class: "w-4 h-4") %>
            <span>Follow</span>
          <% end %>
        <% end %>
        <%= button_to block_user_path(user.username),
              class: "#{outline_classes} opacity-80" do %>
          <%= lucide_icon("ban", class: "w-4 h-4") %>
          <span>Block</span>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
</div>
